<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!--
        WHAT THE TOOLS TAB IS BUILT FROM - `docs/PROJEKT-TOOLS-20261020.md`. Styles only, and not one value
        of its own: every brush, size and margin points at a name the rest of the window already uses.

        A CARD IS THE OVERVIEW'S CARD MADE INTO ONE BUTTON. The same fill, edge, corner and padding as
        OverviewCard, because a page of cards beside a page of cards should not look like two products -
        `docs/11` 9b, copy the pattern rather than invent one (GUI rule 2). One button rather than a border
        with a button inside, because the whole card does one thing: one tab stop, one name for a screen
        reader, one place to click.
    -->

    <!--
        ONE CARD, PRESSABLE. Drawn rather than based on the library's button - nothing of its chrome is
        wanted, the reason ScopePosition gives - so every state is this file's job: hover, the keyboard
        and off are all below.

        HOVER LIGHTS THE EDGE, NOT THE FILL, AND THAT IS ARITHMETIC. The panel's hover fill is
        SurfaceHoverOnPanel, #565656, and the card carries subdued text: #A0A0A0 on it is 2.81 to 1 against
        4.5 for text to be read - measured 2026-10-20 - where the same text on the card's own fill is 4.76.
        The edge goes from SurfacePanelEdge to TextSubdued instead, 4.76 against the fill, so the card is
        visibly the one under the pointer and nothing on it gets harder to read.

        THE FOCUS RING IS THE OVERVIEW'S, a FocusVisualStyle drawn only while the keyboard is in use - the
        repair ScopeBar.xaml made on 2026-08-25 and the overview copied.

        OFF IS SAID BY THE WORDS, NOT BY THE CARD. The text styles below go quiet when the card cannot be
        pressed, and the reason is on the card's tooltip, which shows on a disabled control because the
        view asks for ShowOnDisabled - `docs/10` trap 14.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ToolCard" TargetType="Button">
        <Setter Property="FocusVisualStyle" Value="{StaticResource OverviewFocusRing}" />
        <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Top" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginToolCard}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PadTile}" />

        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                    <Border x:Name="Face"
                            Background="{StaticResource SurfacePanel}"
                            BorderBrush="{StaticResource SurfacePanelEdge}"
                            BorderThickness="{StaticResource BorderAllSides}"
                            CornerRadius="{StaticResource CornerTile}"
                            Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
                        <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Stretch" VerticalAlignment="Top" />
                    </Border>

                    <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                        <MultiTrigger>
                            <MultiTrigger.Conditions>
                                <Condition Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True" />
                                <Condition Property="IsEnabled" Value="True" />
                            </MultiTrigger.Conditions>
                            <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
                        </MultiTrigger>
                    </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE PROBLEM, AS THE CARD'S TITLE. The overview's label size with the weight of a heading, because
        it is the one thing on the card somebody scans for - "is my problem here". Quiet when the card is
        off, and the binding is to the card's own Available rather than up the tree to the button, which
        GUI rule 9 forbids.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ToolTitleText" TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource OverviewLabelText}">
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource TextWeightHeader}" />
        <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap" />
        <Style.Triggers>
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Available}" Value="False">
                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
            </DataTrigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>

    <!-- What pressing it does, under the title. Subdued already, so it has no quieter state to go to. -->
    <Style x:Key="ToolWhatText" TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource BodySubduedText}">
        <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginTileLabel}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE SAME THING FROM A TERMINAL, small at the foot - owner's decision T-h. The fixed face every
        command in this window wears, at the small size and in the subdued colour, because it is a footnote
        to the card and not its subject.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ToolCommandText" TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource FixedText}">
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeSmall}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
        <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginAboveStatus}" />
    </Style>
</ResourceDictionary>
